multigradedRegularity -- computes the minimal elements of the multigraded regularity of a module over a multigraded ring
************************************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        multigradedRegularity(S,I)
        multigradedRegularity(S,M)
        multigradedRegularity(X,I)
        multigradedRegularity(X,M)
  * Inputs:
      * S, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, a multigraded Cox ring
      * X, a *note normal toric variety:
        (NormalToricVarieties)NormalToricVariety,, a product of normal toric
        varieties
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, an ideal over a multigraded
        ring
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, a module over a multigraded
        ring
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * UpperLimit => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value null,
        largest twist to compute cohomology for
      * LowerLimit => a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, default value null,
        smallest twist to compute cohomology for
      * Strategy => a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, default value null,
        implemented strategies are "CohomologySearch" and "TruncationSearch"
        (default)
  * Outputs:
      * a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of multidegrees

Description
===========

Given a module M over a multigraded ring S or a product of toric varieties X,
this method finds the minimal elements of the multigraded Castelnuovo-Mumford
regularity of M as defined in Definition 1.1 of [MS04] (see arXiv:math/0305214
(see https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0305214 )). If the input is an ideal,
multigraded regularity of $S^1/I$ is computed.

There are two strategies implemented and run using *note hooks:
(Macaulay2Doc)hooks,:

Strategy => "CohomologySearch"

  * This strategy calls the *note cohomologyHashTable:
    (TateOnProducts)cohomologyHashTable, method from *note TateOnProducts:
    (TateOnProducts)Top, and checks for the multidegrees where the Hilbert
    polynomial and Hilbert function match and where the higher sheaf cohomology
    vanishes.

Strategy => "TruncationSearch" (default)

  * This strategy uses *note isQuasiLinear: (LinearTruncations)isQuasiLinear,
    method from *note LinearTruncations: (LinearTruncations)Top, to search for
    the multidegrees where the module is regular by checking the Betti numbers
    of the truncation of the module. See Theorem 4.6 of arXiv:2110.10705 (see
    https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.10705 ). This strategy is much faster.

Note that both strategies require the module or ideal to be saturated by the
irrelevant ideal of the Cox ring.

As an example, here we compute the minimal elements of the multigraded
regularity for Example 1.4 of [BES20] (see arXiv:1703.07631 (see
https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.07631 )). We consider the example of a hyperelliptic
curve of genus 4 in $\PP^1\times\PP^2$.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : X = toricProjectiveSpace(1)**toricProjectiveSpace(2)                    |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = X                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : NormalToricVariety                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = ring X; B = ideal X;                                                |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of S                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : I = ideal(x_0^2*x_2^2+x_1^2*x_3^2+x_0*x_1*x_4^2,                        |
|                                                                             |
|             2 2    2 2        2   3      3      3                           |
|o4 = ideal (x x  + x x  + x x x , x x  + x x  + x x )                        |
|             0 2    1 3    0 1 4   1 2    1 3    0 4                         |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Ideal of S                                                              |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|x_0^3*x_4+x_1^3*(x_2+x_3))                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

After saturating the defining ideal by the irrelevant ideal we may compute its
multigraded regularity.

+------------------------------------+
|i5 : J = saturate(I,B);             |
|                                    |
|o5 : Ideal of S                     |
+------------------------------------+
|i6 : L = multigradedRegularity(X, J)|
|                                    |
|o6 = {{2, 2}, {4, 1}, {1, 5}}       |
|                                    |
|o6 : List                           |
+------------------------------------+

If *note debugLevel: (Macaulay2Doc)debugLevel, is larger than zero, additional
information about the degree search is printed.

This method also accepts the ring provided by *note productOfProjectiveSpaces:
(TateOnProducts)productOfProjectiveSpaces, from the *note TateOnProducts:
(TateOnProducts)Top, package.

Contributors
============

Lauren Cranton Heller contributed to the code for this method.

Caveat
======

The input is assumed to be saturated. Moreover, if the input is a module
generated in non-positive degrees, then the output may be incorrect. In that
case, adding the optional argument

LowerLimit => apply(n, i -> min(degrees M / (deg -> deg_i))) - dim X

where M is the module and X is the toric variety, may be a sufficient solution.

Ways to use multigradedRegularity:
==================================

  * "multigradedRegularity(NormalToricVariety,Ideal)"
  * "multigradedRegularity(NormalToricVariety,Module)"
  * "multigradedRegularity(Ring,Ideal)"
  * "multigradedRegularity(Ring,Module)"

