randomCurveP1P2 -- creates the ideal of a random curve in P^1xP^2
*****************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        randomCurveP1P2(d,g,F)
        randomCurveP1P2(d,g)
  * Inputs:
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, degree of the curve.
      * g, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, genus of the curve.
      * F, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, base ring.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Attempt: Attempt, => ..., default value 1000, limit number of
        attempts for randomCurveP1P2
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, defining random curve
        $\PP^1\times\PP^2$ from a curve of degree d and genus g in $\PP^3$ over
        F.

Description
===========

Given a positive integer d, a non-negative integer g, and a ring F,
randomCurveP1P2 produces a random curve of bi-degree (d,d) and genus g in
$\PP^1\times\PP^2$. This is done by using the *note curve: (SpaceCurves)curve,
function from the *note SpaceCurves: (SpaceCurves)Top, package to first
generate a random curve of degree d and genus g in $\PP^1\times\PP^2$, and then
applying *note curveFromP3toP1P2: curveFromP3toP1P2, to produce a curve in
$\PP^1\times\PP^2$.

Since *note curveFromP3toP1P2: curveFromP3toP1P2, relies on projecting from the
point $[0:0:0:1]$ and the line $[0:0:s:t]$, randomCurveP1P2 attempts to find a
curve in $\PP^3$, which does not intersect the base locus of these projections.
If the curve did intersect the base locus the resulting curve in
$\PP^1\times\PP^2$ would not have degree (d,d). The number of attempts used to
try to find such curves is controlled by the *note
randomCurveP1P2(...,Attempt=>...): Attempt, option, which by default is set to
1000.

+-----------------------------------------+
|i1 : randomCurveP1P2(3,0);               |
|                                         |
|               ZZ                        |
|o1 : Ideal of ---[x   ..x   , x   ..x   ]|
|              101  0,0   0,1   1,0   1,2 |
+-----------------------------------------+
|i2 : randomCurveP1P2(3,0,QQ);            |
|                                         |
|o2 : Ideal of QQ[x   ..x   , x   ..x   ] |
|                  0,0   0,1   1,0   1,2  |
+-----------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

This creates a ring $F[x_{0,0},x_{0,1},x_{1,0},x_{1,1},x_{1,2}]$ in which the
resulting ideal is defined.

